RENTKARO
IMPLEMENTATION PLAN
Local Things Rental Platform
Prepared by: Yasir Ali
DAE CIT
 
1. Introduction
This implementation plan explains how the RentKaro project can be developed step by step. RentKaro is a local rental platform where owners can list items and renters can search for items, choose rental dates, and send booking requests. The plan is based on the features described in the RentKaro project presentation and documentation.
2. Implementation Objectives
•	Build a clear and user-friendly rental platform.
•	Allow users to register and log in.
•	Allow owners to add and manage items.
•	Allow renters to search items and send booking requests.
•	Allow owners to accept or reject requests.
•	Provide booking history, reviews, and admin management.
3. Implementation Phases
Phase 1: Project Planning
Estimated duration: Week 1
•	Review the project requirements and confirm the main features.
•	Decide which features are essential for the first version.
•	Prepare a simple page layout and user flow for renter, owner, and admin.
Phase 2: UI/UX Design
Estimated duration: Week 2
•	Design the home page, login page, item listing page, item details page, and dashboards.
•	Choose readable colors, fonts, and responsive layouts.
•	Review the design on mobile and desktop screen sizes.
Phase 3: Database and Backend
Estimated duration: Weeks 3–4
•	Choose a suitable technology stack and database.
•	Create data structures for users, items, categories, bookings, and reviews.
•	Add authentication and role-based permissions.
•	Create backend operations for saving and retrieving listings and bookings.
Phase 4: User Accounts and Item Listings
Estimated duration: Week 5
•	Build registration and login.
•	Allow owners to add, edit, and manage item listings.
•	Support item photos, descriptions, condition, price, location, and availability.
Phase 5: Search and Booking
Estimated duration: Weeks 6–7
•	Add search and filters for category, location, price, and availability.
•	Build item details and rental-date selection.
•	Allow renters to send requests.
•	Allow owners to accept or reject requests and show booking status.
Phase 6: Reviews and Admin Panel
Estimated duration: Week 8
•	Add ratings and reviews after a rental.
•	Build admin tools for users, listings, categories, reports, and complaints.
•	Check that admin-only features are protected.
Phase 7: Testing and Fixes
Estimated duration: Week 9
•	Test registration, login, listing creation, search, and booking requests.
•	Test owner approval and rejection.
•	Check mobile, tablet, and desktop layouts.
•	Fix errors and check that saved data remains available after signing out and back in.
Phase 8: Deployment
Estimated duration: Week 10
•	Prepare the production build and hosting environment.
•	Configure the database and environment settings securely.
•	Run a final test before sharing the platform with users.
•	Prepare a short user guide and basic maintenance plan.
4. Suggested Project Timeline
Phase	Duration	Main Result
Planning	Week 1	Confirmed requirements and workflow
UI/UX Design	Week 2	Page designs and responsive layout
Database and Backend	Weeks 3–4	Database and core server operations
Accounts and Listings	Week 5	Login and item management
Search and Booking	Weeks 6–7	Main rental flow
Reviews and Admin	Week 8	Feedback and management tools
Testing	Week 9	Tested and corrected application
Deployment	Week 10	Ready-to-share first release
Note: The 10-week schedule is an estimate for planning purposes. Actual timing depends on the selected technology, available development time, and project requirements.
5. Suggested Technology Selection
The project presentation does not specify a programming language, framework, or database. Before development starts, choose a stack that matches the developer's skills and hosting options. The same platform could be implemented with a web frontend, a backend/API, and a relational or document database. Do not treat any particular stack as already selected.
6. Testing Plan
Test Area	What to Check
Account testing	Confirm that users can register, log in, and access only the features allowed for their role.
Listing testing	Confirm that owners can add and update item information and that renters can view listings.
Search testing	Check that filters return relevant items based on the selected criteria.
Booking testing	Check request submission, owner decisions, status updates, and overlapping-date protection.
Review testing	Confirm that reviews are allowed only according to the project's review rules.
Responsive testing	Check pages on phone, tablet, and desktop widths.
Data testing	Confirm that information is saved correctly and remains available after a new login.
Security testing	Check permissions, form validation, and safe handling of user data.
7. Expected Results
•	A responsive platform for renters, owners, and administrators.
•	A working process for listing items and searching available items.
•	Booking requests with owner approval or rejection.
•	Organized booking status and history.
•	Basic ratings, reviews, and admin moderation.
•	A tested first release that can be improved in later versions.
8. Future Work
•	Online payments, if required and supported.
•	Email or in-app booking notifications.
•	Map-based nearby search or location services.
•	Owner verification and improved complaint handling.
•	Additional languages and accessibility improvements.
9. Conclusion
The implementation plan divides RentKaro into manageable stages, from planning and design to backend development, booking features, testing, and deployment. Following these stages can help keep the work organized and make it easier to identify problems before the platform is released.
